<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <em id="bib.bib39.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">First Conference on Language Modeling</em>.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Full Results</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Evaluation on Other LLMs</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the generality of our approach, we additionally apply Ours (+GA) to two other LLMs: Qwen2.5-7B Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and Gemma2-9B Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rivière et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.
We follow the same evaluation protocol as described in the main experiments and evaluate CU metrics (NodeAcc, EdgeAcc, and their non-target counterparts) and general utility.
In this experiment, we evaluate all methods on the <span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Wiki-Fact</span> corpus and compare Ours (+GA) against two representative baselines: ICU and GA on the <span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Wiki-Fact</span> corpus.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results are shown in Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.1 Evaluation on Other LLMs ‣ Appendix A Full Results ‣ Concept Unlearning in Large Language Modelsvia Self-Constructed Knowledge Triplets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
Ours (+GA) consistently achieves complete unlearning for Gemma2.
While Ours (+GA) cannot achieve complete forgetting in Qwen2.5, it shows strong suppression for the forgetting target.
In addition, compared to GA on the <span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Wiki-Fact</span> dataset, our method achieves substantially higher non-target preservation and general utility, confirming the selective forgetting property.
For example, in the case of Qwen2.5, GA achieves complete unlearning but suffers significant collateral damage (i.e., NodeAcc (others): 11.5% and EdgeAcc (others)= 11.1%), whereas Ours (+GA) maintains much higher non-target accuracy (i.e., NodeAcc (others)=48.7% and EdgeAcc (others)= 67.6%).
A similar trend is observed for Gemma2, where Ours (+GA) outperforms ICU and GA in preserving utility while achieving complete forgetting.
These results align well with the main experiments on Mistral and Llama3.1, demonstrating that our method generalizes effectively across LLM architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also show the evolution of CU metrics during the GA-based unlearning process on Llama3.1, Qwen2.5, and Gemma2.
Figure <a href="#A1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ A.1 Evaluation on Other LLMs ‣ Appendix A Full Results ‣ Concept Unlearning in Large Language Modelsvia Self-Constructed Knowledge Triplets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the results of naive GA applied to the <span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Wiki-Fact</span> corpus.
It shows a similar trend to Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Sentence-based loss: Unlearning with Self-Generated Sentences ‣ 4 Proposal ‣ Concept Unlearning in Large Language Modelsvia Self-Constructed Knowledge Triplets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> that EdgeAcc for the forgetting targets decays only gradually, and accuracy for both targets and non-targets declines simultaneously as unlearning progresses.
Therefore, we can confirm that the naive GA on the <span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Wiki-Fact</span> corpus lacks selectivity.
On the other hand, Fig. <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ A.1 Evaluation on Other LLMs ‣ Appendix A Full Results ‣ Concept Unlearning in Large Language Modelsvia Self-Constructed Knowledge Triplets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> demonstrates that Ours (+GA) reduces EdgeAcc for the forgetting targets rapidly, and the accuracy degradation for non-targets is modest, suggesting that our method achieves effective CU on different LLM architectures, as with the main results in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Sentence-based loss: Unlearning with Self-Generated Sentences ‣ 4 Proposal ‣ Concept Unlearning in Large Language Modelsvia Self-Constructed Knowledge Triplets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
